00:19:33,913 --> 00:19:39,913
The Belle of Amherst is a one-woman play by William Luce.

6
00:23:28,695 --> 00:23:34,695
Based on the life of poet Emily Dickinson from 1830 to 1886, and set in her Amherst, Massachusetts, home, the 1976 play makes use of her work,
diaries, and letters to recollect her encounters with the significant people in her life – family, close friends, and acquaintances.

7
00:27:23,478 --> 00:27:29,478
It balances the agony of her seclusion with the brief bright
moments when she was able to experience some joy.

8
00:31:18,260 --> 00:31:24,260
After one preview, the original Broadway production, directed by Charles Nelson Reilly and starring Julie
Harris, opened on April 28, 1976, at the Longacre Theatre, where it ran for 116 performances.

9
00:35:13,043 --> 00:35:19,043
The Wall Street Journal reviewer wrote "With her technical ability and her emotional range, Miss Harris can convey profound inner turmoil at the same time that she displays irrepressible gaiety of spirit."
Harris, who portrayed
15 different characters in the play, won the Tony Award for Best Actress in a Play, earned a Drama Desk Award nomination for Unique Theatrical Experience, and won a Grammy Award for Best Spoken Word Recording.

10
00:39:07,826 --> 00:39:13,826
She appeared in a televised PBS production and extensively toured
the country with the play for a number of years.

11
00:43:02,608 --> 00:43:08,608
Dickinson biographer Lyndall Gordon criticized the play for perpetuating Mabel Loomis Todd's chaste, hermit-like image of Dickinson, as opposed to
the lively, witty, provocative, and sometimes erotic Dickinson present in her work and known to those who knew her more personally.

12
00:46:57,391 --> 00:47:03,391
The Spanish translation of the play was done by Argentinean poet Silvina Ocampo for
the Buenos Aires premiere starring China Zorrilla in January 1981.

13
00:50:52,173 --> 00:50:58,173
The production ran for more than 500 performances and Zorrilla embarked on a Latin American tour
that ended at New York's Hunter College in 1983 and Washington's Kennedy Center.

14
00:54:46,956 --> 00:54:52,956
With Emily, the Uruguayan actress made a triumphant comeback to her
country after 10 years of proscription for political reasons.

15
00:58:41,739 --> 00:58:47,739
In 1999, the play was presented by Cesear's Forum at
Playhouse Square, Cleveland, Ohio.

16
